From 546cd4bf00a53c78ce2ab4f35eaf34188edb2a1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 23:04:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] swapstack: move the stack in assembler; a __stack
 definition is enough

__stkinit kept the new stack's top in A2 across __MyStackSwap, which
loads A2 (and A6) without saving them. __SaveSP was then moved by
&stack - stk_Upper instead of the distance the stack moved, and exit()
returned through garbage (Software Failure 80000004, or no return).

The move is now one assembler routine, __stkmove(lower, upper,
&__SaveSP, top): it copies the stack from sp up to tc_SPUpper below
the new top, swaps
tc_SPLower/tc_SPUpper and sp under Disable(), adds the distance to
__SaveSP (and to A5 when a frame pointer points into the moved stack),
and saves every register it uses. No register, frame or pointer chosen
by the compiler lives across the switch. __stkexit moves back the same
way, copying only what lies below __SaveSP (the old stack above it was
never touched), then frees the new stack.

Defining __stack did nothing unless the program also named __stkinit,
because nothing pulled swapstack.o out of the library. Every start code
now references __stkinit, and a default __stack of 0 (keep the stack)
sits in an object of its own, which a program's own definition
replaces. So `unsigned long __stack = N;` alone gives the program N
bytes of stack when it was started with less, from a Shell or from
Workbench.
